To be completely honest, I'm not sure how you could integrate it into your existing repo. However, what you could try is cloning, then copy-pasting all of your files into `packages/core/integration-tests/test/integration/webext`. Then replace the code inside `packages/core/integration-tests/test/webext.js` with the following:
```js
import path from 'path';
import {ncp} from '@parcel/fs';
import {prettyDiagnostic} from '@parcel/utils'
import {bundle, assertBundles, inputFS, outputFS, distDir} from '@parcel/test-utils';

describe('webext', function() {
  it('should resolve a full webext bundle', async function() {
    try {
      let b = await bundle(
        path.join(__dirname, '/integration/webext/manifest.json'),
      );
      await ncp(outputFS, distDir, inputFS, './webext-out');
    } catch(e) {
      for (const diag of e.diagnostics.map(prettyDiagnostic)) {
        console.error(diag.message);
        console.error(diag.codeframe || diag.stack);
        for (const h of diag.hints) console.error(h);
      }
    }
  });
});
```
Run `yarn test:integration -g webext` in the root directory. You should see your web extension in `packages/core/integration-tests/webext-out`.

@mischnic There are two things I'm having trouble with automating: preventing the server from running and adding support for source maps. While the server running doesn't make it impossible to use the transformer, it's certainly a waste of resources. Source mapping only works inline in web extensions, so I need a way to force source maps to be inline, but `addDependency` does not support `target` properly.

In addition, there's the issue that HMR doesn't work without an explicit HMR host for content scripts because they default to `window.location` for HMR, which in the case of content scripts isn't the same as the HMR server. For example, if I match `*://github.com/*`, HMR tries to connect to `https://github.com:1234/`. Even if I manually set the host, on secure domains it tries to access `wss`, which fails.

Beyond those (non-critical) features, this PR is ready for merging.

1. To resolve the "someone has a file named manifest.json that isn't a web app manifest" situation and the workarounds that are needed to make this work, we should instead have a `@parcel/config-webextension` with something like this:
```js
{
  "extends": "@parcel/config-default",
  "transformers": {
    "manifest.json": ["@parcel/transformer-webextension"],
    "raw:*": ["@parcel/transformer-raw"]
  }
}
```

Then the current json transformer doesn't have to be modified. We plan to properly fix the "every JSON is transformed to JS" in the near future (then that `raw:` pipeline could be removed entirely).

2. Is `isEntry` fine at the moment for files specified in manifest.json? I think hashes don't provide any benefit here anyway?

We use it to inject scripts into the page "context" since content scripts cannot access all properties of the `window` object. We usually use it along the lines of 
```javascript
// Inject the page context script
const script = document.createElement("script");
script.src = browser.runtime.getURL("content/page/page-context.js");
if (document.documentElement) {
  document.documentElement.appendChild(script);
}
```

